"""
Two line-breaking rules the engine did not have: `white-space: pre` preserves
newlines as forced breaks, and `overflow-wrap: normal` forbids breaking INSIDE
a word.

**Item 1 — `white-space: pre`.** `white_space_nowrap` carried both `nowrap` and
`pre`, and the `#text` branch gave that one flag a single wrap range spanning
the whole run. That is right for `nowrap` (which COLLAPSES newlines) and wrong
for `pre` (which PRESERVES them), so a `<pre>` block was one line however many
rows it held. `pre` now has its own `Style.white_space_pre`, set by the UA rule
for `<pre>` and by `white-space: pre`, and the layout splits at every U+000A.
On the catalog's `<pre>` sample this was Chrome 128 against Simple 104, and
because the row sits two thirds of the way down `html.html` its 24 px shortfall
dragged 44 later root rows up behind it — Sigma 1080 attributed, the single
largest item in the 8-page catalog.

**Item 2 — `overflow-wrap: normal`.** The wrap loop cut the line at whatever
byte stopped fitting. CSS Text 3 reserves that for `overflow-wrap: break-word`
/ `anywhere` and `word-break: break-all`; under the initial value a word wider
than the line OVERFLOWS it. The damage was worst when the run STARTED mid-line,
because then `max_width` is a pen remainder of a few pixels and the
`endv == start` arm emitted one CODEPOINT per line: the catalog's
`<p style="width:80px">LongWord<wbr>BreakHere</p>` came out NINE lines tall
against Chrome's two (dh 168, the largest single delta in the catalog).

Every number below is HARVESTED from Chrome 152 `--headless=new
--window-size=900,20000` over exactly the markup this spec feeds the engine
(`HEAD` reproduces the fixture's `<head>` byte for byte), not predicted:

| case                                             | Chrome h |
|--------------------------------------------------|----------|
| `<pre>A\nB</pre>`                                | 48       |
| `<pre>\nA\nB</pre>` (leading newline dropped)     | 48       |
| `<pre>A\nB\n</pre>` (no trailing empty line)      | 48       |
| `<pre>One line</pre>`                            | 24       |
| `white-space:nowrap` over the same two rows       | 24       |
| `width:80px`, one unbreakable word                | 24       |
| ... plus `overflow-wrap:break-word`               | 48       |
| `width:80px`, four short words                    | 72       |
| a plain-text div in the same block                | 24       |

The last four rows are CONTROLS, and they are why the two rules can be stated
separately: the `nowrap` row fails the moment `white_space_pre` is folded back
into `white_space_nowrap`, and the `break-word` row fails the moment the
`may_break_in_word` condition is deleted rather than inverted.

TWO RESIDUES, stated rather than absorbed:

1. `<wbr>` is still not a soft-break OPPORTUNITY. With the fix
   `LongWord<wbr>BreakHere` is one overflowing line where Chrome makes two, so
   the catalog row moves from +168 to -24 rather than to 0. That is a
   different layer (the inline pen, not the per-run wrapper) and is filed as
   `doc/08_tracking/bug/wbr_not_a_soft_break_opportunity_2026-09-14.md`. The
   discriminator is already measured: `LongWord<i></i>BreakHere` with no `wbr`
   is ONE line in both engines, so the run boundary alone is correctly not a
   break opportunity — only `<wbr>` is missing.
2. `compute_style_wrap_ranges_float_band` keeps the old intra-word chop. It is
   a separate copy of the loop with its own two-width contract, no catalog page
   exercises it, and changing it unmeasured would be a guess.
"""

"""
Traceability: REQ-BROWSER-ENGINE-LAYOUT-015 — a `white-space: pre` run breaks at every preserved newline, and a run under `overflow-wrap: normal` never breaks inside a word.
"""
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/browser_engine/simple_web_html_layout_renderer_layout.spl 1%
use std.spec.*
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_html_layout_renderer.{
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id
}

val VW: i32 = 900
val VH: i32 = 20000
val HEAD: text = "<html><head><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;font:16px/1.5 sans-serif}</style></head><body>"

fn probe(inner: text, id: text, prop: text) -> text:
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id(HEAD + inner + "</body></html>", VW, VH, id, prop)

describe "white-space:pre preserves newlines and overflow-wrap:normal never splits a word":
    it "AC-1: a two-row <pre> is two lines tall":
        step("Chrome 48; the pre-fix engine returned 24, one line for both rows")
        expect(probe("<div><pre id=\"p\">Column A\nColumn B</pre></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("48")

    it "AC-2: a newline immediately after the <pre> start tag is dropped":
        step("Chrome 48, not 72 — the HTML parsing rule, harvested not recalled")
        expect(probe("<div><pre id=\"p\">\nColumn A\nColumn B</pre></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("48")

    it "AC-3: a run ending in a newline does not open a trailing empty line":
        step("Chrome 48, not 72")
        expect(probe("<div><pre id=\"p\">Column A\nColumn B\n</pre></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("48")

    it "AC-4 (control): a one-row <pre> is still one line":
        step("Chrome 24; the newline split must not invent a line where there is no newline")
        expect(probe("<div><pre id=\"p\">One line</pre></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-5 (control): white-space:nowrap over the same two rows stays ONE line":
        step("Chrome 24 — nowrap COLLAPSES newlines, which is why pre needed its own flag")
        step("this is the row that fails if white_space_pre is folded back into white_space_nowrap")
        expect(probe("<div><div id=\"p\" style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Column A\nColumn B</div></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-6: an unbreakable word wider than its box overflows on ONE line":
        step("Chrome 24; the pre-fix engine chopped it into 2 lines")
        expect(probe("<div><p id=\"p\" style=\"width:80px\">LongWordBreakHere</p></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-7 (control): overflow-wrap:break-word still breaks inside the word":
        step("Chrome 48 — intra-word breaking is narrowed to where CSS asks for it, not removed")
        expect(probe("<div><p id=\"p\" style=\"width:80px;overflow-wrap:break-word\">LongWordBreakHere</p></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("48")

    it "AC-8 (control): ordinary multi-word wrapping is untouched":
        step("Chrome 72 — four short words in an 80 px box still break at the spaces")
        expect(probe("<div><p id=\"p\" style=\"width:80px\">alpha beta gamma delta</p></div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("72")

    it "AC-9 (control): a plain-text div in the same block keeps its single line":
        step("Chrome 24 — neither rule may reach a run that has no newline and no long word")
        expect(probe("<div id=\"p\">plain text</div>", "p", "h")).to_equal("24")
